# Aesthetic mappings: aes

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])

alias Giggly.Plot

mpg = Giggly.Datasets.mpg()
mtcars = Giggly.Datasets.mtcars()

render = fn plot ->
  plot
  |> Giggly.to_svg()
  |> IO.iodata_to_binary()
  |> Kino.Image.new(:svg)
end
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`aes()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/aes.html), in the order the page gives them.
Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly cannot draw yet keeps
its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list of what remains.

An aesthetic mapping says which of the data's columns each visual property of a layer's marks, its
aesthetics, is drawn from. ggplot2 builds one with `aes()`, which quotes its arguments, so each is
an expression evaluated in the data, and standardises the aesthetics' names. A mapping given to
`ggplot()` applies to every layer, and one given to a layer to that layer alone. A Giggly mapping is
a plain map from aesthetic to column, `%{x: :displ, y: :hwy}`, a column named by an atom or a
string; it names columns only, not expressions or constants. The mapping given to
`Giggly.Plot.new/2` applies to every layer, and a layer's own mapping is laid over it. A plot is
described with `Giggly.Plot` and drawn with `Giggly.to_svg/2`, which returns an SVG document. The
setup cell aliases `Giggly.Plot`, so the cells write `Plot.new/2`, and defines `render`, which draws
a plot and shows it below the cell with `Kino.Image`. The examples use `Giggly.Datasets.mpg/0`,
ggplot2's `mpg`: fuel economy for 234 cars of 38 popular models from 1999 to 2008, and
`Giggly.Datasets.mtcars/0`, R's `mtcars`: 32 cars from the 1974 *Motor Trend* US magazine, which the
setup cell binds to `mpg` and `mtcars`, the names ggplot2 and R give them.

## A mapping

```r
aes(x = mpg, y = wt)
aes(mpg, wt)
```

Each call builds the same mapping, which R prints; `x` and `y` may go unnamed. A Giggly mapping is
a plain map, `%{x: :mpg, y: :wt}`, given to `Giggly.Plot.new/2` or to a layer; no function builds
it, and every aesthetic is named, `x` and `y` included.

## Expressions of columns

```r
# You can also map aesthetics to functions of variables
aes(x = mpg ^ 2, y = wt / cyl)
```

Not in Giggly yet: an expression in a mapping, such as `mpg ^ 2` or `wt / cyl`.

## Constants

```r
# Or to constants
aes(x = 1, colour = "smooth")
```

Not in Giggly yet: an expression in a mapping, here the constants `1` and `"smooth"`, and mapping
`colour`. A string in a Giggly mapping names a column, as an atom does.

## Standard names

```r
# Aesthetic names are automatically standardised
aes(col = x)
aes(fg = x)
aes(color = x)
aes(colour = x)
```

ggplot2 rewrites each of these names to `colour`. Giggly takes `colour` and `color` as two
spellings of one aesthetic, and refuses a mapping that gives both; it does not translate `col` and
`fg`, names from R's base graphics. Not in Giggly yet: mapping `colour`.

## The plot's mapping or a layer's

```r
# aes() is passed to either ggplot() or specific layer. Aesthetics supplied
# to ggplot() are used as defaults for every layer.
ggplot(mpg, aes(displ, hwy)) + geom_point()
ggplot(mpg) + geom_point(aes(displ, hwy))
```

A mapping given to the plot is used by every layer:

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new(%{x: :displ, y: :hwy})
|> Plot.geom_point()
|> render.()
```

Given to the layer instead, it draws the same plot:

```elixir
mpg
|> Plot.new()
|> Plot.geom_point(%{x: :displ, y: :hwy})
|> render.()
```

## A wrapper that takes dots

```r
# Tidy evaluation ----------------------------------------------------
# aes() automatically quotes all its arguments, so you need to use tidy
# evaluation to create wrappers around ggplot2 pipelines. The
# simplest case occurs when your wrapper takes dots:
scatter_by <- function(data, ...) {
  ggplot(data) + geom_point(aes(...))
}
scatter_by(mtcars, disp, drat)
```

Giggly's mappings name columns by value, an atom or a string, so a wrapper passes them on as it
would any other value, and nothing needs quoting. Here the wrapper takes a whole mapping, as the
dots do.

```elixir
scatter_by = fn data, mapping ->
  data
  |> Plot.new()
  |> Plot.geom_point(mapping)
end

mtcars
|> scatter_by.(%{x: :disp, y: :drat})
|> render.()
```

## A wrapper with named arguments

```r
# If your wrapper has a more specific interface with named arguments,
# you need the "embrace operator":
scatter_by <- function(data, x, y) {
  ggplot(data) + geom_point(aes({{ x }}, {{ y }}))
}
scatter_by(mtcars, disp, drat)
```

Named columns are passed one by one, again as plain values, with no operator.

```elixir
scatter_by = fn data, x, y ->
  data
  |> Plot.new()
  |> Plot.geom_point(%{x: x, y: y})
end

mtcars
|> scatter_by.(:disp, :drat)
|> render.()
```

## Functions in a wrapper's arguments

```r
# Note that users of your wrapper can use their own functions in the
# quoted expressions and all will resolve as it should!
cut3 <- function(x) cut_number(x, 3)
scatter_by(mtcars, cut3(disp), drat)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `cut_number()`, and an expression in a mapping, such as `cut3(disp)`.
